From: lmoresi <louis.moresi@anu.edu.au>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:51:47 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] add_fault keeps a vertex on each turn; round-3 review:
 seam edges counted once

Turns. A sample where the trace turns by more than 45 degrees is pulled
after the plain samples (the tips still last), so it keeps the vertex it
shares with a neighbour, and that vertex is labelled as the tips' are; the
chain must pass through it. Before, a turn that lost its vertex to the
next sample left the cut across the corner: kink120_2 on the regular box,
sampled half a cell apart, was 0.5 cells off at the corner and its slip
33 % low (now 0 and 3 %). On the 480-trace box grid: faults more than
10 % off among those added 22 -> 13, and the coverage distance now has a
gap, every one-piece fault within 0.172 cells of its trace or at 1.25 or
more (150-degree hairpins that lose an arm), so half a cell no longer
decides anything at round-off. Development-placed faults whose mesh
changes: 13 of 232, one vertex each.

Review round 3 (Linux workstation):
- the chain's edges are gathered and counted ONCE: an edge on a partition
  seam is held by both its ranks, and counted twice its ends looked
  interior, so a stray end on a seam was accepted on 8 of 9 layouts;
  pieces, ends and turns all use the one deduplicated set;
- an earlier slit is recognised by the exact side labels of the faults
  already split into the mesh, not any label ending in Plus or Minus;
- a chain that fails a check beside such a slit is no longer passed: the
  refusal is handed back and raised after the record check and the split,
  whose own messages come first;
- the tip and turn labels are removed after use;
- the parallel test runs add_fault distributed as well, catches every
  exception before its gather, and refuses a stray end on a seam.

test_0897: a turn keeps its vertex (fails before: 0.5 cells), a turn
vertex off the chain is refused, a label named like a slit is not one.
Suites: 433 passed, 1 skipped. The parallel test is not run here.

